## Context

Severed from the multilingual brief of 2026-09-02 (DECISIONS.md, ratified
entry of that date) by the author's ruling: detecting and tagging
translations, creating translations, and splitting bilingual records are a
different beast from retrieval. The question under them is the one this
ticket names, and it is a spinoff from the semantic retrieval workshop this
repository is. Nothing here is scheduled; the label keeps it off the work
queue. It exists so the thinking is not lost.

## The three questions, and the brief's answers as a starting position

Not rulings. The author has not ruled on them and this repository will not.

- **Detect and autotag translations; which is the original?** The brief's
  position: detect as a report (candidate pairs with confidence and evidence),
  never write into Zotero; the original declared by the user through Extra's
  CSL variables `original-title` / `original-date` plus a Related link, both
  of which Zotero already understands, so no new field is needed. Facts: Zotero
  has no translation relation predicate; the language field is set on 6 663
  of 13 287 records in twelve spellings (`bench/results/0573-lang-census.txt`).
- **Create translations?** The brief's position: no; the standing ruling keeps
  translation models off the default path, and the consuming agent translates
  on demand. A management tool might see this differently.
- **Autosplit bilingual records?** The brief's position: no; the record is one
  work filed once, and the 113 "EN, VN" language values say the author means
  it. What retrieval needs from such records (index both attachments) stays in
  tracker 0573 as the prerequisite of its parallel gate.

## Added by the author, 2026-09-02: three more management questions

Curation tasks of the same kind, each a write into the user's records and
so the spinoff's, none of them rulings:

- **Uniform language tags.** The census found the language field spelled
  twelve ways for three languages (`en`, `EN`, `en-US`, `English`, `Anglais`,
  `eng`, `VN`, `vi`, `vi-VN`, "EN, VN" …; `bench/results/0573-lang-census.txt`).
  A management tool would propose one form (BCP-47 is the natural target,
  and Zotero's own citation processor expects it) and apply it on
  confirmation. The retrieval side only normalizes the value it stores.
- **Uniform author forms.** One person filed several ways: initials against
  full given names, particle and capitalization variants, order of family
  and given names (Vietnamese and Hungarian names in particular), with and
  without diacritics. Detection is a clustering over the creators table;
  the write is a merge of forms the user confirms, the way Zotero's own
  duplicate merge works for items and does not for creators.
- **Re-diacritics.** Vietnamese titles, names and abstracts typed or
  imported without their diacritics ("Nguyen Van A", "Ha Noi"). Restoring
  them is a solved problem in the language-technology literature (a
  sequence-labelling or n-gram model per language) and a write the user
  must confirm field by field, since the restoration is ambiguous for short
  strings such as names. Retrieval already folds diacritics on both sides
  (R19), so this is a curation question, not a search one.

## If the spinoff opens

It is a different product surface: a writer into the user's library, which
this repository's design has no channel for (the local API is read-only; C1
makes everything zoteus holds derived data). Start from a new requirements
sheet, not from SPEC.md.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Closed when the spinoff opens elsewhere (with the address), or when the
      author declares the question dead.
